  {
    id:'opqb-t22-1', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A patient presented with unilateral ptosis that masks a horizontal squint which becomes obvious when lifting the eyelid. Examination shows limited ocular movement, but the pupillary reflex is normal. What is the most likely cause of this condition?',
    options:['Oculomotor nerve palsy','Diabetic ophthalmoplegia','Myasthenia gravis','Horner syndrome'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The stem is built from the book\'s pupil-sparing third-nerve picture, and the pupil is the whole question. The neuro-ophthalmology algorithm splits acquired third-nerve palsy on exactly this finding: "Pupil spared → Vasculopathy" (diabetes, hypertension) versus pupil involved → compressive lesion (aneurysm, tumour), because "pupillary fibres lie dorsal and peripheral" in the nerve, so an external compression hits them early while a microvascular infarct of the nerve\'s core spares them (ophthalmology.pdf p.182). The Tutorial table gives the clinical dress the stem copies — "Ptosis (masks the diplopia)", limited movement, and a pupil with normal reaction (ophthalmology.pdf p.183) — here the ptosis masks the squint until the lid is lifted. In a question-bank vignette the vasculopathic palsy is the diabetic ophthalmoplegia of option B. "Oculomotor nerve palsy" unqualified is the trap: the stem IS a third-nerve palsy, but as a diagnosis-of-cause option it stands for the surgical, pupil-INVOLVED palsy of the algorithm\'s other branch, which the normal pupillary reflex excludes — the bank wants the aetiology, and the pupil hands it to diabetes. Myasthenia gravis also never touches the pupil — the book prints "THE PUPIL IS NEVER AFFECTED IN MG" (ophthalmology.pdf p.185) — but its ptosis and motility deficit are FATIGABLE and variable, worse at the end of the day, often bilateral; the stem offers none of that, and a fixed unilateral palsy pattern fits an infarcted nerve, not a junction disease. Horner syndrome is sympathetic: MILD ptosis (Müller\'s muscle only) with MIOSIS and no limitation of ocular movements at all — a constricted pupil is an abnormal pupil, and the stem\'s motility deficit rules it out twice over. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Use the spared pupil to place an acquired third-nerve palsy on the vasculopathic (diabetic) branch of the algorithm rather than the compressive one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q1 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf pp.182–183 (pupil-sparing algorithm; Tutorial table), p.185 (MG pupil).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-2', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'The most useful investigation for a patient with a picture of central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO) is:',
    options:['CT orbit','OCT','ERG','Tonometry'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The bank keys OCT. CRVO is a clinical diagnosis — the fundus picture of disc swelling, tortuous veins and haemorrhages in all four quadrants makes it — so the "most useful investigation" question is really about what changes management, and the sight-limiting sequel that needs measuring and following is macular oedema. OCT is the instrument that images and quantifies macular oedema directly and non-invasively (the ranking of OCT as the single most useful test is not taken from the course material — the retinal vascular deck\'s printed follow-up plan for CRVO macular oedema is to wait 6–12 weeks and perform FLUORESCEIN ANGIOGRAPHY, L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders; FA is not on this menu, and the bank\'s key reflects the modern practice of following oedema with OCT). ERG is the strongest distractor: electroretinography genuinely helps grade the ISCHAEMIC subtype of CRVO (a reduced b-wave marks widespread inner-retinal ischaemia — not taken from the course material), but it is a specialised electrophysiology test, not the everyday working investigation. Tonometry matters in the aftermath — ischaemic CRVO threatens rubeosis and neovascular glaucoma, so IOP is watched — but measuring pressure neither confirms the occlusion nor assesses the macula. CT orbit images bone and orbital soft tissue and contributes nothing to a retinal vascular event. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Pick OCT as the working investigation for CRVO macular oedema and place ERG, tonometry and FA in their supporting roles.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q2 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders (CRVO macular oedema follow-up: wait 6–12 weeks, perform FA).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-3', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A positive Seidel test in corneal breaks occurs when:',
    options:['The dye turns blue','The dye turns clear','The dye forms a thick deposit','The dye pools in the fornix'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The trauma lecture defines the sign in one line: the "Dye turns \'clear\' when break present as aqueous dilutes fluorescein" (L25) Trauma I, sl.61). Concentrated fluorescein painted over a suspected full-thickness corneal wound is dark orange-green; if the globe is open, aqueous humour streams out through the break and washes a dilute, bright-flowing rivulet through the dye — under the cobalt blue light the leak fluoresces as a clearing green stream against the dark concentrated background, and that visible dilution is the positive Seidel test declaring an open globe. "The dye turns blue" confuses the dye with the lamp — the fluorescein is viewed UNDER cobalt blue light, but the dye itself fluoresces green and never turns blue. "A thick deposit" is the opposite of what a leak does: aqueous thins and washes the dye away; deposits are what mucus and debris do. "Pooling in the fornix" is the fate of every drop instilled into any eye, leaking or intact — it says nothing about a corneal break. The same fact is tested by opqb-t21-23 as a hammer-injury vignette keyed "Clearness of dye"; this printing is the definitional form with a fully different option menu, so both stand. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recognise a positive Seidel test as fluorescein turning clear where leaking aqueous dilutes it, declaring an open globe.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q3 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.61.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-4', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient with blunt eye trauma shows a tear-drop pupil. What symptom will he complain of?',
    options:['Monocular diplopia','Binocular diplopia','Complete vision loss','Redness only'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The bank\'s printed key is B, binocular diplopia — but the trauma lecture prints the opposite for this exact lesion, and the discrepancy has to be set out. A tear-drop (peaked) pupil after blunt trauma means the iris has been torn: either a sphincter tear distorting the pupil margin, an iridodialysis (the iris root disinserted from the ciliary body, leaving a second opening at the periphery), or — the most dangerous reading — an occult globe rupture with iris plugging the wound, where the peaked pupil points toward the breach. For iridodialysis the lecture is explicit: "Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)" (L25) Trauma I, sl.29) — light entering through both the pupil and the peripheral dialysis hole forms two images in the SAME eye, a diplopia that persists when the other eye is covered. That is monocular diplopia, option A. Binocular diplopia, by contrast, is a symptom of misalignment of the TWO visual axes — a nerve palsy or an entrapped muscle — and vanishes on covering either eye; an iris defect cannot produce it, and nothing in this stem injures a muscle or a nerve. The key contradicts the deck; per this project\'s rule the printed key is recorded as the answer and the contradiction is noted here, not disputed. Complete vision loss is not a feature of an iris tear with clear media. Redness alone understates any globe injury severe enough to distort the pupil — and a peaked pupil should first make the examiner rule out an open globe before cataloguing symptoms at all (L25 sl.49–50). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Link a traumatic tear-drop pupil to iris injury — where the deck teaches UNIOCULAR diplopia — and know this bank keys binocular diplopia against its own source.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q4 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.29 (iridodialysis — uniocular diplopia), sl.49–50 (peaked pupil as an open-globe warning sign).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-5', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A child with asthma and eczema shows allergic conjunctivitis. What is the possible complication?',
    options:['Hypopyon','Shield ulcer','Corneal melt','Retinal edema'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'A child with asthma and eczema is an atopic child, and his allergic conjunctivitis is the vernal/atopic keratoconjunctivitis family — the book lists atopic dermatitis among the associations of this severe allergic eye disease (ophthalmology.pdf p.87). Its characteristic corneal complication is the SHIELD ULCER: the giant cobblestone papillae of the everted upper tarsus abrade the corneal epithelium mechanically, and eosinophil-derived mediators poison healing, producing an oval epithelial defect typically in the upper third of the cornea that becomes coated with a plaque of fibrin and mucus (ophthalmology.pdf p.88) — the plaque is what stops re-epithelialisation and threatens scarring and amblyopia in a child. Hypopyon — a sterile or infective pus level in the anterior chamber — belongs to bacterial corneal ulcers, endophthalmitis and severe iritis, not to allergic disease. Corneal melt (keratolysis) is the peripheral or paracentral stromal dissolution of severe dry eye, rheumatoid and other collagen-vascular disease (not taken from the course material), not of vernal disease. Retinal oedema is a posterior-segment finding with no route from a conjunctival allergy. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Name the shield ulcer as the corneal complication of vernal/atopic keratoconjunctivitis in an atopic child.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q5 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf pp.87–88 (atopic association; shield ulcer).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-6', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'An MRI of the brain shows multiple demyelinating plaques. What symptom is the patient most likely to complain of?',
    options:['Total blindness','Impairment of color vision','Photophobia only','Sudden painful corneal ulcer'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Multiple demyelinating plaques on brain MRI are multiple sclerosis, and the ocular disease that declares MS is optic neuritis — the book names demyelination/MS as the most common cause of optic neuritis, with MS following in about 50% of cases and MRI as the investigation that shows the plaques (ophthalmology.pdf p.190). Among its symptoms, impaired COLOUR vision is the classic early complaint and the one this key wants: dyschromatopsia — washed-out reds — is disproportionate to the acuity loss and is printed both in the book\'s optic-neuritis signs and in the optic-nerve deck\'s list of optic-nerve-disease signs (ophthalmology.pdf p.190; L21) Optic Nerve, sl.3, colour vision reduced). The full picture is subacute unilateral visual loss with pain on eye movement, a relative afferent pupillary defect and reduced colour vision. "Total blindness" overshoots: optic neuritis impairs vision in ONE eye, usually incompletely, and typically recovers over weeks — bilateral total blindness is no part of the picture. "Photophobia only" is the symptom of corneal and anterior-segment inflammation, not of a retrobulbar nerve lesion; the pain of optic neuritis is on eye MOVEMENT. A sudden painful corneal ulcer has nothing to do with central demyelination — the cornea is not myelinated tissue and MS does not ulcerate it. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Tie MRI demyelinating plaques to optic neuritis and pick impaired colour vision as its characteristic visual complaint.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q6 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.190 (optic neuritis — MS, colour vision, MRI); L21) Optic Nerve sl.3.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-7', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'In the previous case — a patient whose brain MRI shows multiple demyelinating plaques — what is the treatment of choice?',
    options:['Topical steroids','Systemic steroids','Oral antibiotics','Antihistamine drops'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The case is the optic neuritis of demyelinating disease, and its treatment of choice is systemic corticosteroid. The book\'s tutorial box gives the regimen precisely: intravenous methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days, followed by oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 11 days, then tapered over 3 days (ophthalmology.pdf p.191) — high-dose systemic steroid shortens the attack and speeds visual recovery, and in a patient whose MRI already shows plaques the same evidence base ties the steroid course to delaying further demyelinating events (the recovery-versus-final-outcome distinction and the MS-delay point are not taken from the course material; the optic-nerve deck itself prints no treatment for optic neuritis — the regimen is the book tutorial\'s). Topical steroids are the trap: the inflamed nerve sits BEHIND the globe, and a drop on the ocular surface reaches the cornea and anterior chamber only — no meaningful drug reaches a retrobulbar optic nerve, which is exactly why the route must be systemic, and intravenous at that. Oral antibiotics treat infection; demyelination is inflammatory, not infective, and no organism is involved. Antihistamine drops treat allergic conjunctivitis — itching and redness of the ocular surface — and have no relation to an optic neuropathy. The bank prints this stem as "In the previous case, what is the treatment of choice?"; the antecedent (its Q6, opqb-t22-6) is restated here so the question stands alone. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat demyelinating optic neuritis with systemic steroid — IV methylprednisolone then tapering oral prednisolone — never with topical drops.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q7 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.191 (tutorial regimen).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-8', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-red-mgmt',
    stem:'A patient with a history of hypertension presents with eye redness. In which situation should you refer immediately to an ophthalmologist?',
    options:['Mild discomfort only','Watery discharge','Symptoms worsening in the evening','History of trauma'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'This is the referral lecture\'s core skill: sorting the red eyes a general physician may watch from the ones that must go straight to the ophthalmologist. Trauma is on the wrong side of that line every time — the lecture\'s history-taking for the red eye asks specifically about a history of trauma, and injury to a red eye raises the possibilities no non-specialist can exclude: an open globe, hyphaema, an intraocular foreign body, traumatic secondary glaucoma (L37) When to Refer non ophthalmologist. The trauma deck gives the reason its weight — a ruptured globe is the most devastating of all eye injuries and must be ruled out before anything else is done to the eye (L25) Trauma I, sl.49–50) — and ruling it out takes a slit lamp and an ophthalmologist. The distractors are the picture of simple conjunctivitis, the red eye that does NOT need referral: mild discomfort (not real pain — pain is the danger signal, discomfort is not) and watery discharge are the everyday features of a self-limiting viral conjunctivitis, safely managed in primary care with hygiene advice. "Symptoms worsening in the evening" is a fatigue pattern — it belongs to eyestrain and to myasthenic ptosis, and it is not among the red-eye danger signals (the danger list runs on pain, visual loss, photophobia, haloes — not the time of day). The patient\'s hypertension is a distractor in the stem itself: a subconjunctival haemorrhage in a hypertensive is alarming to look at but harmless; trauma is what changes the disposition. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Refer every traumatic red eye immediately and manage the mild watery-discharge picture of simple conjunctivitis without referral.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q8 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L37) When to Refer non ophthalmologist (red-eye history and danger signals); L25) Trauma I sl.49–50.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-9', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A patient present with limitation of abduction of the left eye and horizontal diplopia. What is the compensatory head posture?',
    options:['Head tilt to the same side','Face turn to the same side','Head tilt to the opposite side','Face turn to the opposite side'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Limited abduction with horizontal diplopia is a lateral rectus (sixth nerve) palsy, and the compensatory posture follows the squint lecture\'s rule: the head moves in the direction of action of the paralysed muscle, so the eyes need not (L30) Squint II, sl.7). The paralysed left lateral rectus acts in LEFT gaze; turning the FACE to the left (the same side as the lesion) carries the eyes into right gaze relative to the head — out of the field of the dead muscle — and the diplopia collapses. The book prints it for the sixth nerve exactly: an abnormal head position with face turn to the SAME side of the lesion to minimise diplopia (ophthalmology.pdf p.184, with "same" underlined in print). A face turn to the OPPOSITE side would drive gaze toward the paralysed muscle\'s field and make the diplopia maximal — the exact reverse. The two head-TILT options belong to a different axis altogether: tilts compensate for TORSIONAL and vertical imbalance, classically the fourth-nerve (superior oblique) palsy, where the head tilts AWAY from the affected side (ophthalmology.pdf p.183); a purely horizontal deficit has nothing for a tilt to correct. The same fact is tested by opqb-t15-679, which names a left sixth-nerve paralysis outright and keys the concrete "Turning his face to the left."; this printing stems on the motility finding and offers same-side/opposite-side abstractions instead, so both stand. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Turn the face toward the side of a sixth-nerve palsy — the head moves into the dead muscle\'s field so the eyes can leave it — and keep head tilts for torsional palsies.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.137, Q9 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L30) Squint II sl.7; ophthalmology.pdf pp.183–184.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-10', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient present with unilateral tearing, redness, and discharge for 1 day. What is the most important sign to evaluate?',
    options:['Conjunctival papilla','Conjunctival follicle','Hypopyon','Relative afferent pupillary defect'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'An acutely red, watering, discharging eye of one day\'s duration is acute conjunctivitis, and the tarsal conjunctiva is where its cause is read. The follicle is the sign the key wants: a follicle is a focal aggregate of LYMPHOCYTES in the conjunctiva — a small, dome-shaped, avascular pale swelling — and follicular conjunctivitis is the signature of VIRAL conjunctivitis (and of trachoma), which is also the commonest acute watery red eye; the book\'s conjunctivitis pages define the follicle and print follicular conjunctivitis in its viral and trachomatous forms side by side (ophthalmology.pdf p.86). Finding follicles in this one-day watery picture clinches a viral aetiology, predicts the contagious self-limiting course, and spares the patient antibiotics. The papilla is the follicle\'s counterpart and the deliberate near-miss: a papilla has a central FIBROVASCULAR core and belongs to bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis (ophthalmology.pdf p.86) — it is a sign worth reading too, but the acute watery unilateral story is viral until proven otherwise, and the follicle is its marker. Hypopyon — pus layering in the anterior chamber — is a sign of corneal ulcer, endophthalmitis or severe iritis; its appearance would mean the diagnosis is not conjunctivitis at all. A relative afferent pupillary defect is an optic-nerve (or gross retinal) sign; conjunctivitis never produces one, and checking pupils in this stem is normal-by-expectation rather than diagnostic. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Evert the lid and read follicles (lymphocytic, viral) against papillae (fibrovascular, bacterial/allergic) in acute conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q10 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.86 (follicle vs papilla; follicular conjunctivitis figure).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-11', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'A 92-year-old female presents with central vision loss and metamorphopsia, consistent with advanced age-related macular degeneration (AMD). Appropriate treatment is:',
    options:['Steroid drops','Anti-VEGF injections','Low vision aids','Pan-retinal photocoagulation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The word doing the work in this stem is ADVANCED. The macular lecture assigns AMD\'s two forms their treatments: dry AMD (about 90% of cases) gets dietary supplements to slow progression, and wet AMD (about 10%) gets intravitreal anti-VEGF injections (L35,36) Insidious visual loss). But anti-VEGF treats ACTIVE neovascular disease — it dries oedema and halts a growing membrane; in a 92-year-old whose disease is advanced, the central retina is already destroyed (a disciform scar or geographic atrophy), there is no active process left to inject against, and no drug restores photoreceptors that are gone. What remains treatable is the patient, not the macula: LOW VISION AIDS — magnifiers, high-add spectacles, lighting, eccentric-viewing training — exploit the peripheral retina AMD always spares and give back reading and independence (the rehabilitation content is not taken from the course material; the deck stops at the two disease treatments). That is why the key steps past option B: anti-VEGF is the right answer for wet AMD caught while active, and the wrong one for end-stage disease. Steroid drops treat anterior-segment inflammation and do not reach or help the macula. Pan-retinal photocoagulation is the treatment of PROLIFERATIVE DIABETIC retinopathy — burning ischaemic peripheral retina to shut down VEGF drive — and would only destroy the peripheral field this patient now depends on. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Reserve anti-VEGF for active wet AMD and manage advanced, scarred AMD with low-vision rehabilitation of the spared peripheral field.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q11 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L35,36) Insidious visual loss (dry ~90% supplements; wet ~10% anti-VEGF).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-12', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A patient with mature cataract needs to be evaluated for possible retinal detachment. Which investigation should be done?',
    options:['Direct ophthalmoscope','Ultrasound','Fluorescein angiography','OCT'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'A mature cataract is a totally opaque lens, and every option except one needs light to pass through it. The cataract-surgery lecture writes the rule into its preoperative evaluation: B-scan ULTRASOUND when the fundus is not visible, to rule out retinal detachment (L18) Cataract surgery). Ultrasound is the one imaging modality on the menu that does not care about media clarity — sound crosses the opaque lens, and a detached retina echoes as a bright mobile membrane in the vitreous, so the surgeon knows before operating whether a clear lens will actually restore vision. The three distractors fail for the same single reason. The direct ophthalmoscope is exactly what the mature cataract defeats: no red reflex, no view of the fundus — the situation that triggers the ultrasound in the first place. Fluorescein angiography photographs dye transit through the RETINAL circulation and needs a clear optical path for both the exciting light and the returning fluorescence; behind a mature cataract it produces nothing. OCT is likewise an optical technique — interferometry of reflected light — and cannot image through an opaque lens. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Order B-scan ultrasound to assess the retina behind a mature cataract — the only listed investigation that does not need clear media.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q12 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L18) Cataract surgery (preoperative B-scan when the fundus is not visible).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-13', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A patient present with a mid-dilated, vertically oval, non-reactive pupil. On examination, which finding is expected?',
    options:['Deep anterior chamber','Normal intraocular pressure','Shallow anterior chamber','Dilated retinal vessels'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The mid-dilated, vertically oval, non-reactive pupil is the pupil of ACUTE ANGLE-CLOSURE GLAUCOMA — the congenital/angle-closure lecture prints exactly that pupil among the attack\'s signs, alongside the finding this key asks for: a SHALLOW anterior chamber with iris bombé (L12) Glaucoma). The two findings are one mechanism. In an anatomically crowded eye the mid-dilated iris blocks the pupil, aqueous trapped behind it balloons the iris forward (iris bombé), the peripheral iris seals the drainage angle, and the anterior chamber — already shallow by build — shallows further; the pressure spike then infarcts the iris sphincter, which is why the pupil sits fixed, mid-dilated and vertically oval. A DEEP anterior chamber is the anatomy of the myopic, angle-closure-proof eye and is the exact opposite of what this attack requires. NORMAL intraocular pressure is excluded by the same slide: the attack\'s IOP is printed as raised to stony-hard levels — the globe feels hard as a stone to palpation — and the pressure is what causes the pain, the corneal oedema and the haloes. Dilated retinal vessels belong to a different vascular story (venous obstruction); the fundus finding of a pressure spike, when the hazy cornea allows any view at all, is arterial pulsation or a congested disc, not the picture asked here (venous-occlusion contrast not taken from the course material). This is the companion of opqb-t11-470, which runs the same attack as an EXCEPT-question over four findings; here the bank asks for the one expected finding, so both stand. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Read the mid-dilated vertically oval fixed pupil as acute angle closure and expect a shallow anterior chamber with stony-hard pressure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q13 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L12) Glaucoma (attack signs: pupil, shallow AC/iris bombé, stony-hard IOP).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-14', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A patient with a history of infant fingernail trauma presents with severe pain and photophobia. What is the most common presenting symptom?',
    options:['Redness only','Severe pain','Epiphora only','Vision loss'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'An infant\'s fingernail across the eye is the classic cause of CORNEAL ABRASION — a scraped-off patch of corneal epithelium, delineated by fluorescein staining under blue light (L15,16) Cornea). The dominant symptom is SEVERE PAIN: the cornea is the most densely innervated surface tissue in the body (its rich trigeminal supply is the point of the corneal reflex), and stripping its epithelium exposes those nerve endings directly — pain out of all proportion to the size of the lesion, with photophobia, blepharospasm and tearing riding on it (the innervation-density fact is not taken from the course material). Note the bank\'s construction: the stem itself states "severe pain and photophobia" and the key then answers "Severe pain" — the question restates its own vignette, a vocabulary-of-the-obvious check rather than a discrimination; recorded here, not a defect to dispute. The distractors are the accompaniments mistaken for the headline: redness and epiphora (reflex tearing) are both present in an abrasion but as secondary, reflex phenomena — "only" is what makes each wrong, since neither occurs alone in this injury. Vision loss is not the presenting complaint of a peripheral epithelial scrape; acuity suffers only when the abrasion crosses the visual axis, and even then blurring is overshadowed by the pain. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Expect severe pain as the presenting symptom of a fingernail corneal abrasion and confirm the epithelial defect with fluorescein.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q14 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L15,16) Cornea (abrasion; fluorescein delineation).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-15', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'A diabetic patient undergoes retinal imaging that shows retinal thickening with cystic spaces. The most likely diagnosis is:',
    options:['Retinal tear','Macular edema','Retinoschisis','Hypertensive retinopathy'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Retinal THICKENING with CYSTIC SPACES on imaging in a diabetic is diabetic macular oedema in its cystoid form — the diabetic retinopathy lecture lists cystoid macular oedema among the macular consequences of diabetic leakage (L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy): plasma leaking from incompetent perifoveal capillaries pools in the outer plexiform layer, where it collects into the cyst-like spaces this OCT description names, thickening the retina and degrading central vision. It is the leading cause of visual loss in diabetics, which is why macular thickening is what diabetic screening exists to catch. A retinal TEAR is a full-thickness break, a discontinuity at the peripheral retina — it neither thickens the retina nor forms cysts, and its imaging signature is a flap or hole with vitreous traction. RETINOSCHISIS is the anatomical near-miss: it also produces intraretinal cavities, but by SPLITTING the neurosensory retina into two layers (typically peripherally, in degenerative schisis) rather than by oedema fluid in a thickened macula — and it has no link to diabetes (distinction not taken from the course material). Hypertensive retinopathy writes a different fundus: arteriolar narrowing, arteriovenous nipping, flame haemorrhages, and in severe disease disc oedema — not a cystic, thickened macula as its defining lesion. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Diagnose cystoid diabetic macular oedema from retinal thickening with cystic spaces and separate it from schisis and tears.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q15 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy (cystoid macular oedema).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-16', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'Direct ophthalmoscopy is mainly used to assess:',
    options:['Visual fields','Retinal thickness','Red reflex','IOP measurement'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The examination lecture assigns the direct ophthalmoscope exactly this double duty: assessment of the RED REFLEX and fundus examination (L3,4) Clinical — Ocular examination). Held at arm\'s length, the instrument throws light along the visual axis and the examiner reads the uniform red-orange glow returned from the fundus; any media opacity — a lens opacity above all — interrupts it, appearing as a dark shadow against the red reflex (L3,4 sl.22), which is how cataract is screened at the slit-lamp-free bedside and how leukocoria is caught in a child. Brought close, the same instrument gives the magnified upright fundus view. Of the options offered, the red reflex is the one that is the ophthalmoscope\'s own — each distractor names a task owned by a different instrument. Visual fields are measured by confrontation and by automated perimetry (ophthalmology.pdf p.23); an ophthalmoscope shows the disc that field loss comes from, but it measures no field. Retinal thickness is OCT\'s measurement — cross-sectional interferometry quantifying the macula in microns; ophthalmoscopy sees the surface, not the thickness. IOP is measured by tonometry; no optical view of the fundus reads a pressure. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Assign the red reflex (and fundus view) to the direct ophthalmoscope and fields, thickness and pressure to perimetry, OCT and tonometry.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q16 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L3,4) Clinical — Ocular examination sl.22, sl.49; ophthalmology.pdf p.23.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-17', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient present with conjunctival follicle formation. This is most commonly seen in:',
    options:['Bacterial conjunctivitis','Viral conjunctivitis','Allergic conjunctivitis','Fungal conjunctivitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Follicles are lymphocyte aggregates, and follicular conjunctivitis is most commonly VIRAL — the book prints follicular conjunctivitis in its viral form (with trachoma as the other classic follicular disease) and defines the follicle against the papilla, whose fibrovascular core marks bacterial and allergic inflammation instead (ophthalmology.pdf p.86; the follicle/papilla distinction is set out in full at opqb-t22-10 in this exam). Bacterial conjunctivitis is therefore wrong twice: its purulent discharge is papillary, not follicular. Allergic conjunctivitis is the papillary reaction par excellence — up to the giant cobblestone papillae of vernal disease — driven by mast cells and eosinophils, not lymphoid follicles. Fungal conjunctivitis is a rarity with no follicular signature at all (not taken from the course material). The pairing to memorise is the one the bank keeps testing: follicle = viral (and trachoma/chlamydia); papilla = bacterial and allergic. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Match conjunctival follicles to viral (and trachomatous) conjunctivitis and papillae to bacterial and allergic disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q17 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.86 (follicular conjunctivitis — viral and trachomatous).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-18', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'Which condition can cause a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)?',
    options:['Ischemic optic neuropathy','Conjunctivitis','Cataract','Glaucoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'An RAPD (Marcus Gunn pupil) is the objective signature of an asymmetric AFFERENT lesion — optic nerve above all: on the swinging-light test, the light moved to the diseased side drives less constriction and both pupils dilate. The optic-nerve lecture stars the RAPD among the signs of optic-nerve disease (L21) Optic Nerve, sl.3), and the book lists it among the signs of optic neuritis (ophthalmology.pdf p.190); ischaemic optic neuropathy — infarction of the nerve head — damages the same afferent pathway and produces the same defect, so it is the key. Conjunctivitis is surface inflammation; nothing about a red conjunctiva touches the afferent arc, and the pupils are normal. Cataract is the classic taught negative: even a dense, mature cataract does NOT cause an RAPD — a media opacity scatters light rather than blocking the neural signal, and the retina behind it still fires (this cataract rule is not taken from the course material but is the standard teaching the option tests). An RAPD behind a cataract therefore always means something else is wrong. Glaucoma is the honest near-miss: glaucomatous optic neuropathy CAN produce an RAPD when the damage is markedly asymmetric between the two eyes — but usual glaucoma is bilateral and roughly symmetric, and a symmetric disease produces no RELATIVE defect; the bank keys the condition that produces an RAPD reliably, the acute unilateral optic neuropathy. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Trace the RAPD to asymmetric optic-nerve disease, and know that cataract — however dense — never produces one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.138, Q18 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L21) Optic Nerve sl.3; ophthalmology.pdf p.190.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-19', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Which condition is a contraindication for MRI?',
    options:['Retinal detachment','Metallic intraocular foreign body','Diabetic retinopathy','Glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The trauma lecture prints the rule in capitals: DO NOT DO an MRI scan if a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected (L25) Trauma I, sl.52). MRI is a powerful magnet; a ferromagnetic fragment inside the eye moves in the field, and a fragment that moves inside a globe lacerates retina, lens and uvea on its way — the scan meant to find the injury would complete it. That is why the imaging of suspected IOFB is CT (or plain X-ray), which localises metal safely, and why the trauma history in any machinery, hammering or grinding injury must be taken before any MRI request is signed. The three distractors are ordinary retinal and optic-nerve diseases with no interaction with a magnetic field: a detached retina is neural tissue and fluid, diabetic retinopathy is a microvasculopathy, and glaucoma is a pressure-driven optic neuropathy — none contains metal, and MRI is safe (if rarely needed) in all three. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Never order MRI when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected — localise it with CT instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q19 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.52.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-20', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which test is used to assess visual field in glaucoma?',
    options:['Amsler grid','Light projection','Perimetry','Red reflex'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'Glaucoma is a field disease, and PERIMETRY is the field test. The examination chapter names automated perimetry as the accurate quantitative method of visual-field assessment, usable for both diagnosis and follow-up (ophthalmology.pdf p.23) — exactly what glaucoma care needs, because the diagnosis and the progression of glaucomatous optic neuropathy are read off the field chart. The defects perimetry documents are the ones the POAG lecture lists as the glaucomatous sequence: paracentral (Bjerrum-area) scotomas, the arcuate scotoma arching over fixation along the nerve-fibre bundles, the nasal step, the ring scotoma as arcuates join, and finally the tubular (tunnel) field of end-stage disease with a spared temporal island (L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG; the deck lists the field changes without printing the word "perimetry" — the instrument\'s name comes from the book). The distractors each test something else. The AMSLER GRID examines only the CENTRAL 10 degrees and is the home test of MACULAR disease — metamorphopsia in AMD (ophthalmology.pdf p.23; the macular lecture lists the grid among its macular function tests, L35,36) — far too central to catch glaucoma\'s early arcuate and nasal defects. LIGHT PROJECTION is the crude field-by-quadrants check reserved for eyes seeing LESS THAN 1/60 (ophthalmology.pdf pp.22–23) — a test of whether any field exists at all, not a measurement. The RED REFLEX assesses the clarity of the media (see opqb-t22-16); it says nothing about the field. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Follow glaucoma\'s field defects — Bjerrum, arcuate, nasal step, ring, tubular — with perimetry, and keep the Amsler grid for the central macular field.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q20 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf pp.22–23 (automated perimetry; Amsler grid; light projection); L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG (field-defect sequence).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-21', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'Which drug is used in the management of myasthenia gravis?',
    options:['Anticholinesterase','Beta-blocker','Steroid drops','Cycloplegic'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune destruction of acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, and its symptomatic treatment attacks the arithmetic directly: an ANTICHOLINESTERASE blocks the enzyme that degrades acetylcholine, so each nerve impulse leaves more transmitter competing for the surviving receptors and the fatigable weakness improves. The book\'s myasthenia tutorial prints the drug list this key comes from — treatment may include anticholinesterase drugs, steroids and immunosuppressive drugs (ophthalmology.pdf p.185); the working example is pyridostigmine, and the same drug class given as edrophonium was the classical diagnostic test (drug names not taken from the course material; the course\'s own vignette answer elsewhere is "Neostigmine injections", opqb-t14-621, which tests this same management as a case). A BETA-BLOCKER has no role and is actively hazardous — beta-blockade can worsen myasthenic weakness, and even timolol EYE drops are treated with caution in these patients (not taken from the course material). STEROID drops are the wrong route for the right idea: steroids do treat myasthenia, but as SYSTEMIC immunosuppression per the book\'s list — a topical ocular steroid reaches the surface of the eye, not the neuromuscular junction. A CYCLOPLEGIC paralyses the ciliary muscle and dilates the pupil for refraction and uveitis; in a disease of skeletal-muscle transmission it treats nothing. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat myasthenia gravis with anticholinesterase drugs (with systemic steroids and immunosuppressives as the book\'s further steps), never with beta-blockers.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q21 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.185 (tutorial drug list).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-22', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'The most common type of retinal break is:',
    options:['Exudative','Tractional','Rhegmatogenous','Macular hole'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The key is rhegmatogenous, and the fact behind it is the retinal-detachment lecture\'s: RHEGMATOGENOUS detachment is the most common type of retinal detachment — a retinal tear lets syneretic vitreous fluid seep beneath the retina and strip it off, with ageing vitreous shrinkage, high myopia and trauma as its printed causes (L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations, sl.6). A wording note is owed here: the stem asks for the most common type of retinal BREAK, but the option menu is the menu of DETACHMENT types (exudative, tractional, rhegmatogenous) plus one true break (macular hole) — "rhegmatogenous" (from rhegma, a rent) is the detachment CAUSED BY a break, not a kind of break itself. The bank\'s intended reading is plainly "most common type of retinal detachment", and the key is correct for that reading; recorded, not disputed. EXUDATIVE detachment has NO break at all — subretinal fluid leaks from choroidal tumours or inflammation and shifts with posture. TRACTIONAL detachment also begins without a break: contracting fibrovascular membranes, above all in advanced diabetic retinopathy, pull the retina off mechanically (L20 sl.7). A MACULAR HOLE is a genuine full-thickness break, but at the fovea — a site and mechanism (tangential vitreofoveal traction) far rarer than the peripheral horseshoe tears and holes that drive ordinary rhegmatogenous disease (macular-hole detail not taken from the course material). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Rank rhegmatogenous — break plus vitreous fluid — as the most common retinal detachment, against the break-free exudative and tractional types.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q22 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations sl.6–7.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-23', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Which nerve is most commonly affected in orbital floor fracture?',
    options:['Supraorbital nerve','Infraorbital nerve','Optic nerve','Trochlear nerve'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The INFRAORBITAL NERVE runs in its groove and canal along the orbital FLOOR — the very bone a blow-out fracture breaks — so it is the nerve the fracture catches: the trauma lecture prints infraorbital nerve anaesthesia among the features of the blow-out fracture when the fracture line involves the infraorbital canal (L25) Trauma I, sl.22). The result is the fracture\'s sensory signature: numbness of the cheek, the side of the nose, the upper lip and the upper teeth — a distribution every casualty officer should test with a fingertip on both cheeks whenever periorbital trauma is seen, since it can outlast the bruising. The distractors are placed wrong for a floor injury. The SUPRAORBITAL nerve exits at the orbital RIM above the eye — it is at risk in supraorbital rim wounds, not floor fractures. The OPTIC nerve sits at the orbital apex in the optic canal; traumatic optic neuropathy is a rare, severe head-injury event, not the common companion of a floor fracture. The TROCHLEAR nerve supplies the superior oblique and its injury causes vertical diplopia from muscle palsy — whereas the diplopia of a blow-out fracture comes from mechanical ENTRAPMENT of the inferior rectus in the floor defect, not from any nerve palsy (the entrapment mechanism is tested at opqb-t18-754; the fractured bone itself at opqb-t18-722). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Test infraorbital (cheek) sensation in every orbital floor fracture — the nerve in the floor\'s canal is the one the fracture involves.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q23 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.22.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-24', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A child presents with buphthalmos due to congenital glaucoma. The treatment is:',
    options:['Topical beta-blockers','Laser therapy','Surgical intervention','Observation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'Congenital glaucoma is a SURGICAL disease — the glaucoma lecture\'s management slide says it in the structure itself: medical treatment is TEMPORARY, a holding measure until surgery, and the definitive options are all operations — goniotomy when the cornea is clear enough to see the angle, trabeculotomy or trabeculectomy (with antimetabolites) when it is not, and a drainage-valve implant for refractory disease (L12) Glaucoma). The logic is anatomical: the child\'s angle is malformed (trabeculodysgenesis), the obstruction is a structural membrane over the drainage tissue, and no drop dissolves anatomy — the surgeon incises the angle and restores outflow. Buphthalmos itself is the argument for urgency: an infant sclera stretches under pressure, so the globe enlarges, the cornea widens and clouds with Haab striae, and every week of raised pressure adds irreversible optic-nerve damage and amblyopia. TOPICAL BETA-BLOCKERS are therefore only the bridge the lecture describes — they lower pressure a little while surgery is arranged, and are never the treatment. LASER THERAPY is not among the printed options for congenital disease — laser trabeculoplasty belongs to adult open-angle glaucoma and iridotomy to angle closure; neither addresses a dysgenetic infant angle (laser-role detail not taken from the course material). OBSERVATION is the one indefensible answer: an untreated buphthalmic eye goes blind. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Operate on congenital glaucoma — goniotomy or trabeculotomy/trabeculectomy — using medical treatment only as the bridge to surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q24 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L12) Glaucoma (congenital glaucoma management: medical temporary till surgery; goniotomy/trabeculotomy/trabeculectomy/valve).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-25', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'Which condition can be associated with lower intraocular pressure (IOP)?',
    options:['Open-angle glaucoma','Acute iridocyclitis','Angle-closure glaucoma','Retinal detachment'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The uveitis lecture prints the fact the key tests: in acute anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) the IOP is "usually decreased but may be normal or increased" (L13,14) Uveitis, sl.17). The inflamed ciliary body is the eye\'s aqueous factory, and inflammation shuts the factory down — ciliary shock reduces aqueous secretion and the pressure falls; the same slide\'s caveat matters clinically, because inflammatory debris clogging the trabecular meshwork, or steroid response, can swing the pressure the other way later in the course. Both glaucoma options are excluded by definition: OPEN-ANGLE glaucoma is a chronic optic neuropathy with raised (or at least damaging) pressure from outflow resistance, and ANGLE-CLOSURE is the stony-hard acute pressure spike — each is the disease OF high IOP, not low. RETINAL DETACHMENT is the honest second answer: a detached eye is also classically hypotonous — subretinal fluid and reduced aqueous production drop the pressure a few millimetres below the fellow eye (not taken from the course material) — so "can be associated" is true of option D as well; the bank keys the iridocyclitis its own lecture prints the pressure line for, and the key stands as printed with the overlap recorded here. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Expect a LOW pressure in acute iridocyclitis — the inflamed ciliary body under-secretes — while knowing it may be normal or raised.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q25 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L13,14) Uveitis sl.17.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-26', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which investigation is used to diagnose glaucoma?',
    options:['OCT','Fluorescein angiography','Perimetry','Red reflex testing'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'Glaucoma is diagnosed by demonstrating its optic neuropathy and the field loss that neuropathy causes, and PERIMETRY is the investigation that demonstrates the field loss — automated perimetry is the accurate quantitative field test used for assessment and follow-up (ophthalmology.pdf p.23), and the glaucomatous defect sequence it charts (Bjerrum-area scotomas through arcuate, nasal step and ring to the tubular field) is set out with sources at opqb-t22-20, this exam\'s companion question on the same instrument. Perimetry both makes the functional diagnosis and measures progression, which is why every glaucoma clinic runs on field charts. OCT is the strongest distractor and a genuinely used tool: imaging the peripapillary retinal nerve fibre layer and disc detects structural glaucomatous thinning, sometimes before the field changes — but as a modern structural ADJUNCT to the diagnosis (its glaucoma role is not taken from the course material); against the classical menu the bank wants the functional test. FLUORESCEIN ANGIOGRAPHY photographs retinal circulation and dye leakage — diabetic retinopathy and vein occlusion territory, no role in glaucoma. RED REFLEX testing screens the clarity of the media for cataract and leukocoria (opqb-t22-16); glaucoma leaves the media clear and the reflex normal. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Make and follow the glaucoma diagnosis with perimetry — the functional field test — keeping OCT as a structural adjunct and FA out of the disease entirely.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q26 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.23 (automated perimetry); L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG (field-defect sequence — see opqb-t22-20).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t22-27', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient sustains infraorbital fracture. What is the most important advice?',
    options:['Avoid nose blowing','Apply warm compress','Start topical antibiotics','Immediate surgery'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'The bank\'s "infraorbital fracture" is the orbital floor (blow-out) fracture, and the trauma lecture prints this exact instruction for it: the patient must REFRAIN FROM BLOWING THE NOSE (L25) Trauma I, sl.24). The fracture opens a passage between the orbit and the maxillary sinus below; blowing the nose pressurises the sinuses and drives air — and the nasal flora riding on it — up through the defect into the orbit. The immediate result is surgical (orbital) emphysema, the crepitant air-filled swelling of lids and orbit the lecture lists among the fracture\'s features (L25 sl.18), which can spike orbital pressure; the delayed risk is orbital infection seeded from the sinus. One sneeze can convert a stable fracture into a tense, emphysematous orbit, which is what makes this the advice worth the word "important". A warm compress treats a stye; on a fresh fracture it does nothing useful (cold, if anything, for swelling). Topical antibiotic drops wet the conjunctiva only — the infective route here is the sinus, which is why prophylaxis, where given, is SYSTEMIC (antibiotic-route detail beyond the printed advice not taken from the course material). Immediate surgery is not the rule for a blow-out fracture: repair is undertaken selectively and often delayed — for persistent diplopia from entrapment, enophthalmos or a large floor defect — once swelling settles; the emergency in this injury is the advice in option A, not the operation. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Forbid nose blowing after an orbital floor fracture — the sinus communication turns each blow into orbital emphysema and an infection route.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.139, Q27 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 2"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.18, sl.24.'
  }